::: {.g-col-12}
#### [Modelling *Asparagopsis* nitrogen bioremediation efficiency in Australian coastal environments](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolmodel.2026.111623){target="_blank"}
:::

::: {.g-col-6}
![](resources/pub-images/Modelling Asparagopsis (4).jpg){fig-alt="Ecological Modelling snapshot"}
:::

::: {.g-col-6}
Tormey Reimer, Katherine A. Cresswell, Kate M. Fraser, Camille A. White, Scott A. Hadley

*Ecological Modelling*, 2026

* [Published paper](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolmodel.2026.111623)
* [Detailed documentation](https://drtreimer.com/modelling-asparagopsis/)
* [Github repository](https://github.com/stormeyseas/modelling-asparagopsis)

:::
::::

Coastal nitrogen enrichment poses a growing threat to marine ecosystems, and macroalgae cultivation has been identified as a promising bioremediation strategy in Australian coastal waters. Recent interest in cultivating *Asparagopsis* species as additives to livestock feeds presents an opportunity to leverage a commercial industry for ecosystem-based management goals, but the bioremediative potential of these species... [Read more](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecolmodel.2026.111623){target="_blank"}
:::::

::::: {.g-col-12 .g-col-md-6}
:::: {.grid .justify-content-space-evenly .align-items-center}
::: {.g-col-12}
#### [Rapid growth causes abnormal vaterite formation in farmed fish otoliths](https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.148056){target="_blank"}
:::

::: {.g-col-6}
![](resources/pub-images/Rapid growth causes.jpg){fig-alt="Journal of Evolutionary Biology snapshot"}
:::

::: {.g-col-6}
Tormey Reimer, Tim Dempster, Anna Wargelius, Per Gunnar Fjelldal, Tom Hansen, Kevin A. Glover, Monica F. Solberg, Stephen E. Swearer

*Journal of Evolutionary Biology*, 2017
:::
::::

Sagittal otoliths are essential components of the sensory organs that enable all teleost fish to hear and maintain balance, and are primarily composed of calcium carbonate. A deformity, where aragonite (the normal crystal form) is replaced with vaterite, was first noted over 50 years ago but its underlying cause is unresolved. We evaluated the prevalence of vateritic otoliths from two captive... [Read more](https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.148056){target="_blank"}
:::::
::::::

